#[chapter/02/lore]{
  notes: [
    "Boof is a dog guide; echoes are the wish rehearsing futures, not proof of any of them."
    "The Grove of Echoes keeps what the town preferred not to receipt: unspent wants, unsounded notes."
    "The Boonberry Fool debuts in person. He was the honk in the treeline; his genre law is that every trope sampled must be returned changed, with interest."
    "The Fool honks the three-wishes trope back changed: three COMPLETED wishes. A wish isn't granted till it's finished."
    "A rehearsal is not a granting; a granting needs a finisher. Boof records this and continues toward the sand."
    "Echo-wrens nest in pairs in the grove's hollow cedars and sing everything a beat late — the egg's hum, the woodcutters' axes, a dog's sneeze. At the grove's edge, a wren-call means anythi..."
    "The lag-stag is never seen. Its cloven prints press into the moss a beat after it passes and carry no scent at all; woodcutters insist it is white. Its prints stopped beside the egg's sto..."
    "Rehearsal moths are the grove's almanac: dusk moths whose wings carry tomorrow's weather, a little smudged. Woodcutters read them before felling and are wrong about a third of the time. T..."
    "Torch-ants will not settle where a light comes back to them twice, so the grove's roots stay cold and its wild rows flicker. Grove workers add, over birch-sap tea, that hob-deer no taller..."
    "Try it again, a little changed: the woodcutters' word for what the grove does when it repeats you a beat late. Not lying; rehearsing. They say it at the saw-pit to a cut that binds, and k..."
  ]
}

